"Building a Solar System: teaching notes",
"Building a Solar System",
"Astro {#}",
"A collapsing cloud keeps its spin (angular momentum), so it spins faster as it shrinks. Gas cannot fall closer to the Sun than the distance where its spin keeps it in orbit, and gas falling from above and below meets in the middle: the cloud becomes a spinning disk. No spin, no disk.",
"The disk is hot near the young Sun and cold far out. Rock and metal are solid almost everywhere; water ice only beyond the frost line, a few AU out. Beyond it there is about four times as much solid material.",
"Planetesimals join into bodies that stop growing when they have swept up their own ring of the disk (the isolation mass): about a tenth of an Earth mass near {#} AU, a few Earth masses beyond the frost line. Cores of about {#} Earth masses pull in gas and become giants, but only if they form before the gas is gone, within a few million years.",
"Radioactive aluminum {#} (half life {#} million years) melted the planetesimals that formed early and were large enough to keep the heat in; their iron sank to cores, the source of iron meteorites.",
"Every stage is a stated model (the Lab section's \"The model and its formulas\" and the list below). <strong>Blind</strong> hides the predictions: the disk radius, the frost line and the isolation mass. Each stage runs on its own clock; To the end jumps to the end of a run. Readings from different stages go to different tables in the worksheet, so students clear the readings between stages.",
"<strong>Our solar nebula.</strong> A cloud {#} AU in radius turning once every {#} million years falls in within about {#} {#} years. Follow the tracked parcel: it ends {#} AU from the Sun, going around once every {#} years, {#} {#} times faster than the cloud turned. Switch to Flat: edge on, the disk is flat.",
"<strong>Slow spin, Fast spin, No spin.</strong> Four times slower spin, {#} times smaller disk; twice the spin, four times the disk. With no spin every parcel falls onto the Sun.",
"<strong>Frost line, today's Sun.</strong> Drag the probe outward and watch which solids appear: rock and metal from {#} AU, iron sulfide from {#} AU, water ice from {#} AU. Compare with the planets marked along the bottom.",
"<strong>Frost line, bright young Sun.</strong> Four times the light: every temperature rises by {#}^({#}/{#}) = {#} and the frost line moves twice as far out.",
"<strong>Minimum mass disk.</strong> Run it to the end in the Flat view: the rocky bodies stop just above the isolation mass curve, {#} to {#} Earth masses; beyond the frost line they reach several Earth masses, but none gets to {#} Earth masses while the gas lasts. Far out, growth is too slow even in {#} million years.",
"<strong>Three times heavier disk</strong> makes a giant beyond the frost line; <strong>Gas gone early</strong> ({#} million years) does not.",
"<strong>Iron cores.</strong> Early planetesimal melts, then Late planetesimal stays solid and Small planetesimal stays solid; Vesta sized grows a core, as Vesta did.",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> In Our solar nebula, use the readouts to find how many times faster the disk's edge goes around than the cloud did. Check it against (r₀/R_d)², and explain why they agree.",
