"<strong>Growth.</strong> Oligarchic growth (Kokubo and Ida {#}, Icarus {#}, {#}; {#}, ApJ {#}, {#}): the big bodies end up about b = {#} mutual Hill radii apart, so each stops at M_iso = ({#}π a² b Σ)^({#}/{#}) ({#}/3M☉)^({#}/{#}), and the time to grow to mass M is T_grow = {#} × {#}⁵ yr (M/{#}²⁶ g)^({#}/{#}) (Σ₁/{#} g cm⁻²)^(−{#}) (a/{#} AU)². The demo starts with {#} planetesimals spaced evenly in log a from {#} to {#} AU, each holding the solids of its ring, and joins neighbors closer than {#} mutual Hill radii once the growth time to their joint mass has passed, keeping mass and angular momentum. Because whole bodies join, the final bodies are between {#} and {#} times M_iso (tested). A body that reaches {#} Earth masses (Mizuno {#}; Pollack et al. {#}) while the gas lasts is marked a giant; how much gas it gathers is not modeled, and neither are the later giant impacts, the giants' pull on their neighbors or migration. The gas lasting {#} to {#} million years spans what young clusters show (Haisch, Lada and Lada {#}).",
"<strong>Iron cores.</strong> A ball of rock formed all at once at time t_f, heated by ²⁶Al and cooled by conduction to a surface held at {#} K; {#} shells, implicit time steps, enthalpy for the melting. Values from Johansen et al. ({#}, A&amp;A {#}, A74): initial heating {#} × {#}⁻⁷ W/kg falling as e^(−t/{#} Myr), c = {#} J/(kg·K), conductivity {#} W/(m·K), rock melting from {#} K to {#} K with {#} × {#}⁵ J/kg; density {#} kg/m³ assumed. The iron sinks once the center is half molten ({#} K). A big body keeps all its heat, so it forms a core if it forms before {#} Myr × ln({#}/{#}) = {#} Myr. Left out: porous starting material (which conducts poorly and melts more easily), melt rising and stirring (a molten shell loses any further heat), ⁶⁰Fe and impacts, and growth over time. The iron core is drawn in Vesta's proportion (core radius {#} of the body's; Russell et al. {#}), scaled by how much of the body got half molten."
